<h2>The question</h2>

<p><a href="/backlog/3427-design-an-operation-manager-a-real-execution-chokepoint-ever/">#3427</a> captures a
2026-08-31 discussion under one framing: WE needs a real execution chokepoint <em>"every command — not only
dispatched-agent commands — routes through,"</em> with four properties: (1) semantically-named operations,
never raw commands; (2) logged and telemetered, even for cheap/read-only calls; (3) tiered by cost
(free-inline / CPU-scheduled / mutating-runner-only); (4) a catalog that grows from real usage, not up-front
enumeration. The card itself says none of this is settled. This survey asks the prior question a prep pass
owes before authoring forks: <strong>how much of this is already built and already ratified, and what is
actually still open?</strong></p>

<h2>Finding — three of the four bullets are already closed; two narrow axes remain</h2>

<p>The operations engine this card describes already exists, under epic #3029, and has been growing since
before this card was opened. Reading its own shipped code and the statute anchors it composes with answers
three of #3427's four bullets directly:</p>

