<p>What's left, after subtracting the three closed bullets, is narrower than the card's own language ("every
command… not only dispatched-agent commands") suggests: (A) whether the catalog's <em>candidacy scope</em> is
"delivery-loop operations" (#3031's literal text, growing organically) or something wider ("every command,"
literally), and (B) whether the deliberate choice to skip telemetry for cheap/read-only calls should be
revisited. Two more filed items sit adjacent to this ground and must not be re-decided by it:
<a href="/backlog/3188-should-an-agent-session-be-restricted-to-declared-operations/">#3188</a> (still open —
should an agent SESSION be restricted to declared-operations-only, a prompt-injection blast-radius question
for a different population) and <a href="/backlog/3398-conveyor-supervisor-runner-residency-has-no-out-of-band-aler/">#3398</a>
(a filed story — out-of-band alerting specifically for the conveyor supervisor/runner PROCESS's liveness, a
narrower target than "every operation call").</p>

<h2 id="tiers">Cost tiers — the three-tier framing maps onto machinery that already exists</h2>

<p>#3031 clause 2 closes the engine's step vocabulary at four kinds, and clause 4 names two backend tiers.
Read together against a real declaration (<code>scripts/operations/http-adapter.mjs</code>'s own route-table
comment, which is itself derived purely from step kind), the mapping is close to 1:1 with what the operator's
"three tiers" discussion named:</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Operator's tier</th><th>Existing mechanism</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>free-and-inline</td><td><code>compute</code> step — pure fn, no io, completes inside one
<code>advance</code> sweep (<code>engine.mjs</code>)</td></tr>
<tr><td>CPU-scheduled (needs a model, not the full runner)</td><td><code>judge</code> step — suspends for a
spawned juror; the spawn's cost is recorded on the resume (<code>engine.mjs</code>'s <code>withTelemetry</code>)</td></tr>
<tr><td>mutating-and-runner-only</td><td><code>effect</code> step — declares effects, applied only by the
executor keyed by run+step (idempotent replay); the human-in-loop case the operator's three tiers didn't name
gets its own kind, <code>confirm</code></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>Adding a parallel "operation tier" field alongside step kind would duplicate a closed vocabulary #3031
already argues should stay closed ("an operation that appears to need a fifth kind is a signal to change the
model, not to extend the vocabulary") — there is nothing here to design.</p>

<h2 id="telemetry">Telemetry for cheap calls — the run-store deliberately skips this today, on a stated reason</h2>

<p><code>scripts/operations/http-adapter.mjs</code>'s own header is explicit and already reasoned, not silent:
a <code>compute</code>-only declaration's run <strong>is never persisted</strong> — <em>"a record per
page-load is landfill"</em> — because the run store (<code>we:.operations/runs/</code>) is a session-local
sidecar nothing prunes, and a <code>compute</code>-only run completes inside one sweep with nothing to cross
surfaces with. <code>gate-health.mjs</code>'s own header confirms the same thing from the producer side:
<em>"Both steps are <code>compute</code>, so <code>http-adapter.mjs</code> derives a GET-only surface with no
run records — the path <code>suggest-next</code> already exercises."</em> Today, at least four shipped
operations (<code>gate-health</code>, <code>suggest-next</code>, <code>verify</code>, <code>pr-status</code>)
are all-<code>compute</code> and therefore produce <strong>zero</strong> trace of having been called — not a
run record, and nothing else either; no separate log or counter exists for this path. That absence is exactly the shape #3427 cites as its
motivation (#3398's alerting gap; this same epic's own 2026-08-30 blind-review naming "four overlapping
heuristics" for liveness with none of them agreeing) — a real, present gap, not a hypothetical one.</p>

<p>The question this decision actually owns, narrowed by the above, is not "should we log calls" in the
abstract (uncontroversial) but <strong>where a lightweight call-visibility signal should live without
undoing the run-store's own reasoned "no landfill" design</strong> — persisting a full run record for every
compute call reopens a choice that was made for a stated reason, not an oversight.</p>

<h2>Prior art for the shape — a chokepoint whose catalog grows, kept separate from its access log</h2>

<p>Two well-documented, independent precedents for the surviving shape converge on the same split #3427's own
text half-proposes:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Kubernetes' API server</strong> is the single execution chokepoint every client (<code>kubectl</code>,
controllers, other services) must route through, yet its resource <em>catalog</em> is not enumerated up front —
built-in resources ship with the server and the type catalog grows incrementally via Custom Resource
Definitions, registered as real usage demands them. This is the "one chokepoint, organically-grown catalog"
half of #3427's framing, already a mainstream, load-bearing pattern elsewhere — not a novel risk.</li>
<li><strong>Standard web-server architecture</strong> keeps an ephemeral, cheap, rotated <strong>access log</strong>
(every request, minimal cost, prunable) structurally separate from the <strong>durable application data
store</strong> (a database row per meaningful transaction) — exactly the split the run-store's "landfill" reasoning
above is reaching for without a name: a call-visibility signal is an access-log concern, a persisted run record
is an application-data concern, and conflating the two is what the existing design is refusing to do.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What this changes about #3427's own framing</h2>

<p>#3427's "does this include every <code>npm run</code> invocation" question and its "how is telemetry
stored/queried" question are real, but narrower and more concrete than the card states — both are shaped as
forks in the prepared item, each with a bold default grounded in the citations above, rather than left as open
prose. Full grounding in <code>reports/2026-09-01-operation-manager-chokepoint-scope-and-telemetry.md</code>.</p>
